{"number": 35, "text": "     Mm.\n            Yj               Prefixes        and      word       stress       (2)\n\n                   Ay        In some words  with prefixes, the prefix itself is stressed. In CALD, most of these words are\n                             shown  as having main  stress on the prefix and, in some cases, secondary stress later in the word:\n                                   subway   —                      . superpower  —\n\n                             Most  words  like this are nouns and include:\n                                    'co-driver          ‘counterat,tack           ‘hyperspace            ‘interface\n        |                                                                         ‘hypertext             ‘interchange\n                                    ‘co-star            ‘counterpart              ‘hyperlink             ‘interplay\n      |                                                                           ‘under,current\n                                    ‘subtext            ‘super, structure         ‘undergrowth\n                                    ‘subsoil                                      ‘underwear\n      |                      In these words the syllable with main stress usually has prominence in discourse:\n                                   We  took  the SUBway.             I’m just off to the SUpermarket.\n                   BE   72.  However,  other words with  these prefixes have main stress on a syllable after the prefix. Most\n                             words  like this are adjectives and include:\n                                                        ,counterin 'telligence                           inter'changeable\n                                   co'operate           ,counterpro'ductive                              ,interconti'nental\n     | |\n       |\n                                    sub'standard\n                                                                 pose             ,under'age\n\n                             In these words the syllable with main stress usually has prominence in conversation:\n                                    They have to learn to co-eXIST.        The  climate here is subTROPical.\n\n                             but prominence  may  go on the syllable with secondary stress in some cases (see C).\n                        73.  In many  words with a prefix, there is secondary stress on the prefix, with main stress later in the\n                             word:\n                                    ,impre'cise\n\n                             When  these words  are used in conversation they can have stress shift (see Unit 10B), with the\n                             prefix made prominent  rather than the main stressed syllable. Compare:\n                                    Her answer  was impreCISE.    but: She gave an IMprecise ANswer.\n                                    He’s hyperACtive.   but: I work with HYperactive  CHILdren.\n     |  |                           He disaGREED.     but: He  DISagreed STRONGIy.\n                             I don't think\n                             that's right.\n\n\n                             Here are some  more  words with  prefixes which commonly   have stress shift:\n                                           ,de'code; ,diso'bedient, ,disre'spectful; ,imma'ture, ,impo'lite;\n                                        ,mis'spelt; ,recon'sider, ,repro'duce; ,unac'ceptable, ,unsuc'cessful.\n\n                             However,  some  other words with these prefixes rarely have stress shift, including:\n                             de'fame, dis'honest, im'practical, ,mis'judge, re'place, un' popular.\n\n                   36           English Pronunciation in Use (Advanced)", "char_count": 3717, "mean_conf": 82.54, "word_count": 344, "words": [{"text": "Yj", "x0": 231, "y0": 227, "x1": 481, "y1": 283, "conf": 53, "bold": false}, {"text": "Prefixes", "x0": 562, "y0": 226, "x1": 84